   "description": "A binucleate cardiomyocyte is a heart muscle cell that contains two nuclei instead of the usual one; only a minority of cardiomyocytes do so. Apart from its extra nucleus it has the same striated, branched structure as other cardiomyocytes, and it joins its neighbours at intercalated discs so that it contracts with the rest of the chamber. In a section, look for two oval nuclei, often both near the centre, within one muscle cell outline."
  },
  {
   "keys": [
    "red_blood_cell__002"
   ],
   "description": "A deoxygenated red blood cell is an erythrocyte near the downstream (venous) end of a capillary, having already released much of its oxygen to the heart muscle. Its haemoglobin takes up carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions, and most of the carbon dioxide is converted to bicarbonate inside the cell and carried in the plasma to the lungs. In a section it looks like any other red cell, a bright pink, nucleus-free disc, so it is recognised by its position in the capillary rather than its appearance."
  }
 ],
